"Huntsville has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Huntsville's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Huntsville has set <strong class=\"sig\">30 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 83 that chance alone would give, and <strong>82 record highs</strong>, close to the 83 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (69). Huntsville's all-time high is 111\u00b0F, on July 29, 1930.","t_chg":"Huntsville's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1985.","p_chg":"Across 110 complete years: Huntsville's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +0.28), about +2.4\u00b0F since 1908. Huntsville's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.13 to +0.06) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1985, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.79\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.50 to +1.08) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.85\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.46 to +1.25). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 8 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; July fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Huntsville's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.35 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 54.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1989 (73.58 in); the driest, 2007 (28.65 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}